{"concerns_count":9,"dismissed_concerns":[{"description":"The pd_npu power domain node lacks a pm_qos property, which is normally present for power domains in RK356x to preserve Quality of Service state during power transitions.","locations":[{"file":"arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi","function_or_symbol":"pd_npu","line_range":"515-520","why_this_location_matters":"This is the newly added NPU power domain definition where the pm_qos property is deliberately and safely omitted."}],"reasoning":"The omission of the pm_qos property is intentional and safely handled by the driver. As explained in the commit message, the NPU QoS registers sit behind the NoC, which is gated until the NPU is initialized. Including a pm_qos reference would cause a bus fault when the generic power domain (genpd) core attempts to save QoS state while the domain is powered off but the NoC is gated. The Rockchip pm-domains driver (`rockchip_pd_power_off`) safely handles the absence of pm_qos by checking `pd->num_qos` and bypassing the QoS save routine without error.","type":"Missing Hardware Configuration"},{"description":"Missing `npu-supply` property in `rknn_core_0` despite the dt-binding requiring it.","locations":[{"file":"arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi","function_or_symbol":"rknn_core_0","line_range":"579-599","why_this_location_matters":"The node definition intentionally omits the board-specific regulator."}],"reasoning":"While the `rockchip,rk3588-rknn-core` binding states that `npu-supply` is required, omitting it in the base SoC `.dtsi` file is standard kernel practice since regulators are board-specific. The node is marked `status = "disabled"`, so it will not cause probing or validation failures until explicitly enabled and configured in a board's `.dts` file.","type":"Missing Device Tree Property"}],"dismissed_concerns_count":2,"findings":[{"locations":[{"code_snippet":"compatible = "rockchip,iommu";","file":"arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi","function_or_symbol":"rknn_mmu_0","line":603,"why_this_location_matters":"This is where the incorrect compatible string is defined, binding the v2 hardware to the v1 driver implementation."}],"preexisting":false,"problem":"Incorrect IOMMU compatible string 'rockchip,iommu' is used for the RK3568 NPU IOMMU, which will cause translation faults.","severity":"High","severity_explanation":"The NPU IOMMU node `rknn_mmu_0` specifies `compatible = "rockchip,iommu";`. In the Rockchip IOMMU driver (`drivers/iommu/rockchip-iommu.c`), this compatible string binds to v1 hardware operations (via `iommu_data_ops_v1`). However, the RK3568 SoC features v2 IOMMUs. Configuring a v2 IOMMU with the v1 driver operations will result in incorrect page table programming and translation faults when the NPU attempts DMA. All other IOMMU nodes in `rk356x-base.dtsi` correctly use `rockchip,rk3568-iommu`. This issue is not resolved by any subsequent patch in the series."},{"locations":[{"code_snippet":"assigned-clock-rates = <200000000>, <600000000>;","file":"arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk356x-base.dtsi","function_or_symbol":"rknn_core_0","line":593,"why_this_location_matters":"This is where the inverted clock rates are assigned, directly contradicting the commit message and improperly configuring hardware."}],"preexisting":false,"problem":"The assigned clock rates for the NPU AXI bus and compute core are swapped, severely overclocking the AXI bus.","severity":"High","severity_explanation":"The device tree defines `assigned-clocks = <&scmi_clk SCMI_CLK_NPU>, <&cru CLK_NPU>;` and `assigned-clock-rates = <200000000>, <600000000>;`. This positional mapping incorrectly assigns 200 MHz to the SCMI compute core and 600 MHz to the CRU CLK_NPU (AXI bus). This directly contradicts the commit message, which states the intent to 'assign the CRU CLK_NPU so the NPU AXI bus clock comes up at 200 MHz'. Running the AXI bus at 600 MHz is a massive overclock that is highly likely to cause bus hangs and system crashes, while simultaneously underclocking the NPU compute core. The rates must be swapped to match the `assigned-clocks` order. This issue is not resolved by any subsequent patch in the series."}],"fixes":"","review_inline":"commit a304190b5b1e8ffa602c7c5d7dbdfed3a2c3dcae
